  "text_md": "INTRODUCTION TO PSL2 PHASE TROPICALIZATION 5\n\n![](<2503.09133_pg5_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\nFigure 1. This picture represents the image under VAL of a line tangent to the quadric Q(K).\n\na matrix power series A(t) with det(A(t)) not identically zero power series. Normalize to have det(A(t)) = 1. Then A(t) = tγcB + o(tγ) with γ > 0, and c ∈ C∗. Make change of variables in A(t) giving A˜(t) by replacing each tβ by exp(−βγ−1σ)tαβγ−1, where σ = log(c). This comes from the necessity to obtain the image of the term ctγ in the desired form as tα, and it is an automorphism of K fixing C. Then A˜(t) = tαB + o(tα) ∈ V (K) ∩ PSL2(K) and VAL(A˜(t)) = (α,[B]R∗). □\n\n3. Examples\n\nExample 1. We describe the image under VAL of a particular line L tangent to the quadric Q(K). Consider L ⊂ KP3 parametrized by\n\ntw z\n\n0 t−1w ∈ PSL2 K = KP3, t ∈ K being the uniformizer. The point where L intersects the quadric Q(K) is B∞ =\n\nKP1 ∋ [z : w]  −→ Z(t) =\n\n0 1 0 0\n\ncorresponding to w = 0.\n\nFor all other values of [z : 1] ∈ KP1 and z = ctγ + o(tγ), c ∈ C∗, we have three cases: γ > 1, γ = 1 and γ < 1. Look for the limit at ∞ for z represented by decreasing exponents.\n\n0 ctγ 0 0\n\nIn the first case, the dominant term of Z(t) is\n\n. Projected by VAL\n\non the cone picture of PSL2C = CP3, it is a ray over B∞, each point with an S1 in the circle bundle S.\n\nt ct 0 0\n\n, giving a section of the S\n\nIn the second case, the dominant term is\n\n1 0 0 0\n\n0 1 0 0\n\nbundle over Q(C) with points\n\nand\n\nexcluded.\n\n",
